    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i was thinking about using some reclaimed fir i found, but i'm a bit worried about the quality since it's old timber. what kind of stuff should i actually be looking for when i'm inspecting the beam to make sure it won't fail unexpectedly? like, what are the red flags i should watch out for in the grain or the wood itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When inspecting reclaimed wood, you'll want to look for signs that the wood has sustained previous damage or has not been adequately treated. Red flags include:\n\n\n1. Visible cracks or splits: These can indicate internal stress or damage.\n\n2. Knots or major imperfections: Extensive knots, especially near the ends of beams, can reduce strength.\n\n3. Decay or rot: Dark areas or a musty smell may indicate fungal growth.\n\n4. Sapwood/Fiber saturation: Wood that hasn't been properly dried or treated may be susceptible to decay.\n\n5. Treatment history: Absence of or certifications from wood treatment may be cause for concern.\n\n6. Modulus of Elasticity (MOE): A lower than expected MOE can signal weakened fibers.\n\nIt's always a good practice to have any reclaimed lumber tested by a professional before use, especially for structural purposes.",
